; bfsodium CMACSUBKEYS : the two CMAC subkeys from a key
; NIST SP 800_38B section 6 point 1  and RFC 4493 section 2 point 3
;
; NOTE square brackets are brainfuck loops  so comments use braces for counts;
;
; HAND WRITTEN; block/aes128table block/aes128encrypt block/copy16
; block/copy16back block/moveup16 and block/shl128 are INCLUDED;
;
; IO  in:  key{16}
;     out: K1{16} K2{16}      (32 bytes  K1 first)
;
;   L  = AES_128(key  the zero block)
;   K1 = L doubled in the field
;   K2 = K1 doubled in the field
;
; THIS EXISTS TO BE PINNED  and that is its whole job; aes/cmac128 derives the
; same two values inside itself  where nothing can see them: a tag is sixteen
; bytes and a wrong subkey is a wrong tag with no indication of which half
; went wrong; RFC 4493 section 4 PUBLISHES L  K1 and K2 for its key  so this
; file turns an invisible intermediate into a checked one  the same way
; aes/keyexpand128 does for the schedule and aes/subbytes for the S box;
;
; THE ZERO BLOCK NEEDS NO WRITING; the state is already clear when the cipher
; is entered  so the first thing this file encrypts is sixteen cells it never
; touched; That is worth stating because it looks like an omission;
;
; THE DOUBLING IS NOT THE CIPHER'S FIELD; block/shl128 reduces by 0x87  the
; low byte of the polynomial for GF(2^128)  where aes/xtime reduces by 0x1b
; for GF(2^8); Same shape  different field  and the vectors below are the
; reason that distinction is checked rather than asserted;
;
; TAPE MAP  (home @0)
;   @0x000:0x374  the cipher's frame  exactly as block/aes128encrypt maps it
;   @0x375:0x384  savedkey{16}  read here and copied into the window
;   @0x3c7:0x40b  the block/shl128 frame; the value lives at @0x3c7:0x3d6
;                 which is 885 cells above the state  so block/moveup16
;                 carries the cipher's answer straight into it

; ==== the key ====
R885
  ,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,
; ASSERT ptr=900
L900
; ASSERT ptr=0

; ==== the S box is written into the tape ====
; ASSERT zero 0:884
%%block/aes128table%%
; ASSERT ptr=45
L45
; ASSERT ptr=0

; ==== L is the cipher on the zero block ====
; the key is COPIED  because the schedule spends the window and this file
; has nothing further to do with the key  but the copy costs sixteen bytes
; and leaving a working routine alone costs less than specialising it
R885
%%block/copy16%% 885
; ASSERT ptr=900
L884
%%block/copy16back%% 16
; ASSERT ptr=31
; the state is still clear  which IS the zero block
R14
; ASSERT ptr=45
; ASSERT zero 16:44
; ASSERT zero 46:116
%%block/aes128encrypt%%
; ASSERT ptr=0

; ==== and it goes up to the doubling frame ====
R82
; ASSERT ptr=82
; ASSERT zero 967:1035
%%block/moveup16%% 82
; ASSERT ptr=97
R870
; ASSERT ptr=967

; ==== K1 is L doubled ====
%%block/shl128%% 967
; ASSERT ptr=967
; emitting does not spend a cell  so K1 is still there to be doubled again
  .>.>.>.>.>.>.>.>.>.>.>.>.>.>.>.
; ASSERT ptr=982

; ==== and K2 is K1 doubled ====
L15
; ASSERT ptr=967
%%block/shl128%% 967
; ASSERT ptr=967
  .>.>.>.>.>.>.>.>.>.>.>.>.>.>.>.
; ASSERT ptr=982

; THE FINAL WALK IS SPLIT so that the exit contract is CHECKED  for the
; reason CONVENTIONS section 6 gives
L981
; ASSERT ptr=1
; the window holds the tenth round key and the round constant holds 0x6c
; so neither is claimed; everything this file owns above the table is clear
; except K2  which is the answer
; ASSERT zero 16:44
; ASSERT zero 46:116
; ASSERT zero 983:1035
L1
